{
    "m_SGVersion": 0,
    "m_Type": "UnityEditor.ShaderGraph.StickyNoteData",
    "m_ObjectId": "efe2a0bb5acb4e77bf56b55ce9b07fbb",
    "m_Title": "Intensity: per particle brightness",
    "m_Content": "Reads UV1.x (Custom1.x from the Particle System) and multiplies the finished colour by it.\n\nValues above 1 push the colour into HDR, so it blooms. Values below 1 dim it.\n\nThis is how you make each particle flash and fade on its own curve, without needing a separate material. Animate it with a Custom Data curve on Custom1.x.",
    "m_TextSize": 1,
    "m_Theme": 3,
    "m_Position": {
        "serializedVersion": "2",
        "x": 276.0,
        "y": -1534.0,
        "width": 501.0,
        "height": 444.0
    },
    "m_Group": {
        "m_Id": "e4ac42164a02400392e2eeaa245d73e6"
    }
}

{
    "m_SGVersion": 0,
    "m_Type": "UnityEditor.ShaderGraph.StickyNoteData",
    "m_ObjectId": "f09846831cb442b4964c262f0728883f",
    "m_Title": "Secondary Color: optional 2nd tint",
    "m_Content": "WHAT IT DOES\nLets each particle carry a second colour that appears only in certain parts of the texture. Good for hot cores, foam tips, gradients over lifetime.\n\nHOW IT WORKS\n  UV2.xyz (Custom2) = the secondary colour itself.\n  UV2.w             = how much of it to blend in.\n  Main Tex B        = where it is allowed to appear.\nB x UV2.w becomes the T input of the Lerp inside MainColor.\n\nIF YOU SEE NOTHING\nIf Custom2 is not sent from the Particle System, w is 0, the Lerp stays at the main colour and this whole group does nothing. That is the normal fallback, not a bug.",
    "m_TextSize": 1,
    "m_Theme": 2,
    "m_Position": {
        "serializedVersion": "2",
        "x": 1063.0,
        "y": -2761.0,
        "width": 845.0,
        "height": 527.0
    },
    "m_Group": {
        "m_Id": "650b296e506d49218695d2e619656e07"
    }
}

